from above it looks like a lake but in  fact it's a wetland system known as a  Cienega the landscape is actually hilly  but during the rainy season overflowing  rivers form freshwater meadows  transforming it into a swamp  it's a biotope that's home to an  extraordinary variety of plant life fish  and fauna the people who live here are  used to a landscape that changes with  the seasons there are some 1900 Cienega  in Columbia of varying sizes one of the  largest the Cienega disappered osa is  under threat oh myyy Duran hell is a  fisherwoman she and her seven children  live on the island of barranco Nez the  local fisher folk are well aware that  the Cienega is suffering there used to  be many many more fish huge fish in  general there was much more wildlife but  lots of species have died out they've  just disappeared there are species that  younger generations don't know because  they don't exist anymore there are many  problems by setting the Cienega and most  of them are caused by deforestation  trees cut to make way for palm oil  plantations and the ensuing aridity  jungle has also been replaced by pasture  land for cattle and then there's the  increased frequency of any new events  which dry out trees and reduce the  Cienega water levels nature is  completely out of kilter locals are  looking for solutions  oh my EDA has joined forces with other  Fisher men and women and founded an  association to protect the biotope with  more sustainable fishing practices they  now use smaller nets with a larger mesh  to avoid overfishing and to ensure  stocks can replenish  we've laid out some ground rules we  agreed that if anyone casts a net in the  conservation zone as they have done here  that we'll pull it out sometimes we burn  them a further aspect of local  conservation efforts is targeted  reforestation seeds are gathered and  cultivated in tree nurseries until  they're ready to be planted many  residents have played a role in the  deforestation lots of people here have  destroyed trees to gather firewood they  light bonfires on the water's edge and  grilled the fish they've caught or they  collected to take home to their kitchens  here in La Motta like in most other  parts of the region a lot of people live  from fishing in the afternoon volunteer  conservationists hold a meeting the 13  women and four men have come their aim  is to ensure the survival of the Cienega  a unique biotope and with it their  livelihoods at the air divi divi tree  nursery seeds and saplings of local  plants are cultivated and prepared for  planting they're all useful  this is an vita plant it has to be  cultivated from seedlings it's fruit can  be cooked and drunk as juice we're going  to reintroduce the plant and the Cienega  the conservationists make their own  fertilizer from cow dung sand and banana  shrubs as an incentive the aid  organization TNC pays residents for  their plants the Cienega used to be home  to around three thousand nine hundred  species of plant the fact that so many  have died out has wide-reaching  consequences the Boca Chica fish has  died out because there weren't enough  trees on the riverbanks that's why we  decided to replant trees it means the  fish stocks will regenerate to trees  along the water's edge provide aquatic  life with shade and also nutrition when  the fruit is ripe it falls into the  water and the fish can feed on it so  Laura is another village on the Cienega  here in a side street is a further  example of a local initiative to ensure  food security during the dry season a  household garden  the houses here usually have small yards  where owners have planted fruit trees  vegetables and herds some locals are  also planning to raise animals for their  meat and ducks and chickens for eggs the  houses used to have huge backyards  people weren't aware that they could  grow useful plants in the yard is a way  of economizing they are now learning to  cultivate different types of plants and  about how to use the resources neighbors  discuss what to grow with a view to  trading with each other that way they  maximize the range of produce this  household garden is exemplary the  project is helping locals raise enough  to keep themselves fed even during the  dry season and looming on the horizon is  the next El Nino which will mean less  rain the weather pattern is growing more  and more frequent say the fisher folk  making life even more challenging on the  Cienega  [Music]
 

